#open-question(title: [Do Fibrin(ogen) Microaggregates Accumulate in ME/CFS at All?])[
Whether fibrin(ogen) microaggregate *abundance* separates ME/CFS or Long COVID plasma from healthy plasma is unresolved, and the two literatures disagree directly.

*For elevated abundance.* Platelet-poor plasma from 25 ME/CFS patients carried fibrinaloid microclots at more than tenfold greater area than controls @NunesPlatelets2022mecfs. Long COVID plasma showed fibrinolytic resistance with raised alpha-2-antiplasmin @Pretorius2021LCantiplasmin, and fibrin amyloid microclots with platelet pathology were reported in all 80 patients sampled @Pretorius2022LCmicroclotPrevalence. A flow-cytometric method then reproduced the finding quantitatively @Turner2023FlowCytometry. Each of these studies used visual or instrument-scored abundance as the readout, and the 80-patient series reported no quantified comparison against its control samples.

*Against elevated abundance.* A prospective substudy of 151 never-hospitalized participants (80 breathless Long COVID, 37 non-breathless Long COVID, 34 recovered controls; median 483--596 days post-infection; anticoagulant and antiplatelet users excluded) found microaggregate number, microaggregate area, and the count of aggregates larger than 100 square micrometres comparable across all three groups @Fraser2026LCcoagulation. Fibrin fibre density was 47.9 per 100~micrometres in controls, 43.6 in breathless Long COVID, and 38.0 in non-breathless Long COVID ($p = 0.22$, $n = 55$). Every prespecified coagulation readout in that study was null, including D-dimer, Clauss fibrinogen, von Willebrand factor antigen, soluble thrombomodulin, soluble P-selectin, PAI-1, and 50% clot lysis time.

*Why the disagreement is not yet adjudicated.* The cohorts differ in the way most likely to matter: one is never-hospitalized and ambulant, the others mix hospitalized and ambulatory participants. The assays differ more sharply still. Visual microscopy scoring has no shared calibration standard, no reference range, and no blinded external control, whereas the flow-cytometric method has never been cross-validated against it on the same samples. No study has run both methods on one plasma panel.

*Why this is an open question rather than a claim.* The certainty attached to the abundance claim (0.60 @NunesPlatelets2022mecfs) and to the null (0.51 @Fraser2026LCcoagulation) differ by 0.09, inside the 0.15 band that forbids resolving a conflict by weighted preference. Neither side outranks the other on design alone, so neither can be reported as the finding and the other as a footnote.

*Falsifiable prediction:* A preregistered comparison that runs a blinded, calibrated assay on ME/CFS plasma, matched healthy plasma, and a positive-control plasma with known abundant aggregates (septic or disseminated-intravascular-coagulation plasma) would settle abundance for that assay and population. Equal abundance in ME/CFS and healthy plasma, with the positive control reading high, refutes the abundance claim and leaves only composition. Elevated abundance confined to one severity stratum confirms a restricted claim, not the general one.

*Consequence:* If abundance is not elevated, microclot burden cannot serve as a treatment target in ME/CFS, and the anticoagulation and plasmapheresis rationale loses its premise. If abundance is elevated only in severe or hospitalized subgroups, stratification is required before any trial enrols ME/CFS patients. The composition claim survives either outcome, because post-translational modification of fibrinogen was detectable where bulk protein levels were not @Booyens2026POTSPTM.

*Severity applicability.* The null cohort was ambulant and never hospitalized. Severe and very-severe ME/CFS, housebound or bedbound, is untested; nothing here constrains that stratum in either direction.
] <oq:fibrinogen-microaggregate-abundance>
